        "Their answer was an architectural compromise known as Type Erasure. Under type erasure, generic type parameters—such as the 'T' in Box<T> or the 'E' in List<E>—exist strictly at COMPILE TIME! The Java compiler (javac) uses generic type parameters to perform rigorous static type checking, verifying that you never accidentally insert an Integer into a List of Strings. However, once javac completes type verification, it systematically ERASES all generic type arguments from the compiled bytecode class files!",
        "What does the bytecode actually contain? Unbounded type parameters (like <T>) are erased to java.lang.Object. Bounded type parameters (like <T extends Number>) are erased to their first bounding class (in this case, java.lang.Number). Wherever the calling code retrieves an element from a generic class, the compiler automatically inserts synthetic checkcast bytecode instructions behind the scenes.",
        "Because generic type metadata is erased at runtime, the Java Virtual Machine has NO knowledge of what concrete class 'T' represents when a method executes. This fundamental constraint explains why several common programming operations are strictly prohibited by the Java language specification. For example, you cannot instantiate a generic type with 'new T()', because the JVM does not know what constructor to invoke! Similarly, you cannot create a generic array with 'new T[10]' because Java arrays are reified—meaning they enforce runtime component type checks—while generics are erased.",
